  <p>That is the answer to what has to happen before Chinese New Year in a 618 cycle. On a sixteen-week plan, the Spring Festival holiday falls in the plan's first month. Anything that needs a physical shoot, a casting or a supplier in China has to be booked before the holiday, or the plan loses nine days early and the makeup Saturdays after it.</p>

  <p>The same arithmetic works for Singles Day.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Counted back sixteen weeks from Tmall's 15 October 2025 presale, the plan starts on 25 June 2025.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: date arithmetic, 10 September 2026, on the reported presale opening, not a published lead time.</p>

  <p>On a sixteen-week plan for Singles Day, the start falls in late June, days after the 618 cycle ends. For brands running both, the two plans overlap, and the 618 wrap-up and the Singles Day kickoff compete for the same production capacity.</p>

  <h2>What happens if creative approval slips?</h2>

  <p>No source measures what a late approval or a late reshoot costs, so this page puts no figure on either. The mechanism is worth stating, because both failures run into the same wall.</p>

  <p><strong>Late approval.</strong> On Tmall's 2026 618 rules as reported, presale products had to be live before the warm-up began. An approval that slips past that point does not delay the listing. It removes it from the presale.</p>

  <p><strong>Late reshoot.</strong> A reshoot needs the product, the set and the people again. On a sixteen-week plan for 618, the Spring Festival holiday sits early in the window, so a reshoot discovered after it has less calendar left than the plan's length suggests.</p>

  <p>Both are reasons to lock creative earlier than the rules require, and to treat the rules' publication date as confirmation rather than as the starting gun.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/resources/insights/tmall-product-image-requirements">Tmall product image requirements article</a> covers the listing images each cycle needs, the <a href="/solutions/retailers">retailers solutions page</a> covers running a catalog through both festivals, and the <a href="/services/design/ecommerce">ecommerce design service page</a> covers the production itself.</p>

  <p>Reviewed 10 September 2026. Check each platform's rules for the current year before planning to any date on this page.</p>

  <h2>Questions about Singles Day and 618 timing</h2>

  <h3>When do Singles Day assets have to be locked?</h3>

  <p>Before each platform's opening. For 2025, JD and Douyin opened on 9 October and Tmall's presale opened at 8:00 p.m. on 15 October, with Tmall's rules published only 21 days earlier. No platform publishes a separate asset deadline for Singles Day, and no 2026 dates were announced as of 10 September 2026.</p>

  <h3>How long before 618 should production start?</h3>

  <p>No platform, government notice or survey publishes a lead time. Counting back sixteen weeks from Tmall's reported 21 May 2026 opening gives a start of 29 January 2026, with the Spring Festival holiday falling in the plan's first month. That is date arithmetic on reported dates, not a recommendation from any source.</p>

  <h3>What are the Tmall pre-sale milestones?</h3>

  <p>For Singles Day 2025: a warm-up at 2:00 p.m. on 15 October, deposits from 8:00 p.m. that evening to 5:59 p.m. on 20 October, and the spot sale from 8:00 p.m. on 20 October to 14 November. For 618 2026 the reported rules ran a presale from 21 May to 3 June alongside two spot-sale waves.</p>

  <h3>How does Chinese New Year affect a 618 cycle?</h3>

  <p>The 2026 Spring Festival holiday ran from 15 to 23 February, with two Saturdays worked as makeup days. On a sixteen-week plan counted back from Tmall's reported 21 May opening, that holiday falls about twelve to fourteen weeks out, so shoots, casting and suppliers in China need booking before it.</p>

  <h3>What happens if creative approval slips?</h3>

  <p>No source measures the cost, so none is given here. On Tmall's 2026 618 rules as reported, presale products had to be live before the warm-up began, so an approval that slips past that point takes the listing out of the presale rather than simply delaying it.</p>
